 ## Overview
-AABB Intersection via Face Traversal is a method to determine the entry (front) and exit (back) points of a ray intersecting an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) by explicitly testing the ray against each of the six faces of the box [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike the standard slab method which computes intersection intervals along axes, this approach decomposes the AABB into six planar faces and performs individual ray-face intersection tests [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is distinguished from its siblings by its explicit geometric decomposition into faces and triangles, making it conceptually simpler but computationally more expensive than the slab method [S1, S2, unofficial].
